The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
unittest_data = kmalloc(size + FDT_ALIGN_SIZE, GFP_KERNEL);
...
rc = of_resolve_phandles(unittest_data_node);
if (rc) {
    pr_err("%s: Failed to resolve phandles (rc=%i)\n", __func__, rc);
    rc = -EINVAL;
    goto unlock;
}
```
Reason: When `of_resolve_phandles()` fails, the function jumps to `unlock` and ultimately returns without freeing the previously allocated `unittest_data`, causing a memory leak. Other error paths manually called `kfree(unittest_data)`, but this one did not.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
void *unittest_data __free(kfree) = kmalloc(size + FDT_ALIGN_SIZE, GFP_KERNEL);
...
retain_and_null_ptr(unittest_data);
```
Reason: The `__free(kfree)` attribute automatically frees `unittest_data` when it goes out of scope (including all error paths), eliminating the need for manual `kfree` calls. Then `retain_and_null_ptr()` is used on the success path to transfer ownership of the memory to the device tree and set the pointer to NULL, preventing double freeing when the scope ends.

